       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Peds:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " infants: an antithyroid drug (methimazole) in divided doses TID, sometimes w an PO BB for Sx; older children: BBs such as atenolol or propranolol may control HTN and tachy."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Hyperthyroidism:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " adrenergic Sx may respond; propranolol = used most, atenolol or metoprolol are alt. Thyroid storm: propranolol cuts HR w.in 2–3 h PO, min IV."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Interactions:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " digoxin, clonidine, diltiazem and verapamil add brady (slowed AV conduction); stopping clonidine: w.draw the BB several days first (rebound HTN). Strong CYP2D6 inhibitors (fluoxetine, paroxetine, quinidine) can double metoprolol levels. Higher Epi doses may be needed."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
